 one light-day. Radio waves also travel at the speed of light (186,282 miles per second, or 299,792 kilometers per second), but Voyager 1 is so far away that a message it transmits will take an entire day to reach Earth.",more:"In this interactive visualization, you can choose different views to see how long it takes for Voyager to send a message to Earth. You can quickly see that the speed of light itself is not fast enough for space travel in our solar system, let alone to other star systems. <p> The visualization will start at 1,000 times the speed of light, looking from a top-down perspective at the distance between Voyager 1 and Earth. You can also choose different speeds, or switch the view so that the signal starts from Voyager 1, or see the signal reach Earth at the Deep Space Network station in Spain.</p><p> In this interactive, Voyager 1 transmits a signal lasting four hours, and even at 1,000 times the speed of light, it still takes 86 seconds to reach Earth. If you select the option for 10,000 times the speed of light, it will still take almost 9 seconds! </p><p> In the original Star Trek television series, Warp 10 was defined as 1,000 times the speed of light. You can see that is not fast enough to get anywhere meaningful, since it takes over a minute just to travel from Voyager 1 to Earth even at that speed."},{type:"countdownClock",text:"Time until signal reaches Earth at this speed in this simulation",updateFunction:t=>{const e=c,i=Math.floor(t.pioneer.getTime());return{maxTime:e,timeElapsed:Math.floor(i-l)}}},{type:"buttonsSideBySide",resetTimeOnButtonClick:!1,buttonSections:[{blockId:"speed-select",blockTitle:"Select speed",defaultButtonClickId:"1000c",buttonContent:[{id:"1c",label:"1x speed of light",onClick:async t=>{u.setEnabled(!0),p.setEnabled(!0),E(86400)}},{id:"1000c",label:"1000x speed of light",onClick:async t=>{u.setEnabled(!0),p.setEnabled(!0),E(86)}},{id:"10000c",label:"10,000x speed of light",onClick:async t=>{u.setEnabled(!0),p.setEna